<header><p class="enc-kicker">Person</p><h1 class="enc-title">Joseph Bonaparte</h1><p class="enc-meta"><span class="dates"><a href="/years/1768">1768</a>–<a href="/years/1844">1844</a></span> · <a href="/centuries/19th-century">19th century</a></p></header>
<div class="enc-body"><p class="enc-desc">Joseph Bonaparte was the elder brother of <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/napoleon">Napoleon</a>, who made him king of <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/naples">Naples</a> in 1806. In Naples he abolished feudalism, closed monasteries and introduced French administration. In 1808 Napoleon moved him to the throne of <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/spain">Spain</a>, where he faced the Peninsular War until his defeat at Vitoria in 1813, and <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/joachim-murat">Joachim Murat</a> replaced him in Naples.</p><figure class="enc-fig"><img src="/images/encyclopedia/joseph-bonaparte.jpg" alt="Joseph Bonaparte" width="320" height="492" fetchpriority="high"><figcaption>Image: François Gérard · Public domain · <a href="https://commons.wikimedia.org/wiki/File:Joseph-Bonaparte.jpg" target="_blank" rel="noopener">Wikimedia Commons</a></figcaption></figure></div>
<section class="enc-more"><h2 class="enc-more-h">Early life and background</h2><p>Joseph was born Giuseppe Buonaparte on 7 January 1768 at <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/corte">Corte</a>, then capital of the Corsican Republic, to Carlo Buonaparte and Maria Letizia Ramolino. Corsica was invaded by <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/france">France</a> in the year of his birth. He trained as a lawyer, sat in the Council of Five Hundred and served as French ambassador to the Papal States. In 1799 he helped <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/napoleon">Napoleon</a> overthrow the Directory, and on 30 September 1800 he signed the Treaty of Mortefontaine, a treaty of friendship and commerce between France and the <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/united-states">United States</a>.</p><h2 class="enc-more-h">Reign</h2><p>After Austerlitz, <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/napoleon">Napoleon</a> declared on 27 December 1805 that <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/ferdinand-i-of-the-two-sicilies">Ferdinand IV of Naples</a> had forfeited his kingdom. Joseph nominally commanded the invading army, with Marshal Masséna in effective command. The French crossed the border on 8 February 1806 with little resistance, and Joseph entered <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/naples">Naples</a> on 15 February. On 30 March Napoleon decreed him King of Naples and <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/sicily">Sicily</a>. He kept most <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/house-of-bourbon">Bourbon</a> officials in post and began reforms on the French model: monastic orders were suppressed and their property nationalised, feudal privileges and taxes were abolished with compensation for the nobility, and roads and public works were begun.</p><p>Joseph was moved to <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/spain">Spain</a> in August 1808, and his brother-in-law <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/joachim-murat">Joachim Murat</a> succeeded him in <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/naples">Naples</a>. In Spain he was extremely unpopular, and his opponents called him Pepe Botella for alleged heavy drinking, though he was abstemious. His arrival sparked a great revolt and the <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/peninsular-war">Peninsular War</a>, and his command of the French forces was largely nominal. He ended the <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/spanish-inquisition">Spanish Inquisition</a>, but his foreign birth kept most Spanish people from accepting him. He left the throne after the French defeat at Vitoria in 1813.</p><h2 class="enc-more-h">Death and legacy</h2><p>After 1815 Joseph went to the <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/united-states">United States</a>, and from 1817 to 1832 lived mainly there, at Point Breeze, an estate at Bordentown, <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/new-jersey">New Jersey</a>. He was elected to the American Philosophical Society in 1823. He moved to <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/london">London</a> in 1832 and died in <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/florence">Florence</a> on 28 July 1844. His body was returned to <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/france">France</a> and buried in Les Invalides. Joseph Bonaparte Gulf in <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/australia">Australia</a> is named after him.</p><p class="enc-more-src">This account follows the Wikipedia article “Joseph Bonaparte” (CC BY-SA 4.0), linked under Read further below.</p></section>
<section class="enc-quotes"><h2 class="enc-section-title">Across the Institute</h2><ul><li><blockquote>In 1806 Napoleon's armies drove Ferdinand back to Sicily, where he reigned under British protection, while the mainland became a French satellite under <b>Joseph Bonaparte</b>, who abolished feudalism, and from 1808 under Joachim Murat, who modernised the administration and law.</blockquote><a href="/empire/kingdom-of-naples">Kingdom of Naples · Decline &amp; Fall</a></li><li><blockquote>The Junta swore loyalty to Ferdinand VII and rejected <b>Joseph Bonaparte</b>'s puppet government, but refused the Council of Regency of Spain and the Indies, in which no Spanish American had taken part, and sent circulars asking the cities of the former viceroyalty for deputies.</blockquote><a href="/empire/united-provinces-of-the-rio-de-la-plata">United Provinces of the Río de la Plata · Foundation</a></li><li><blockquote>French armies and their allies carried it into Italy, where <b>Joseph Bonaparte</b> abolished feudalism in the Kingdom of Naples in 1806 and the Sicilian parliament followed in 1812; Prussia began freeing its serfs in 1807, Austria abolished peasants’ labour dues in 1848 and Russia emancipated its serfs in…</blockquote><a href="/government/feudalism">Feudalism · Impact &amp; Legacy</a></li><li><blockquote>King Ferdinand IV fled to Sicily under British protection, and Napoleon made his brother <b>Joseph Bonaparte</b> king of Naples.</blockquote><a href="/state/kingdom-of-naples-1806-1815">Kingdom of Naples (1806–1815)</a></li></ul></section>
<section class="enc-where enc-born"><h2 class="enc-section-title">Born and died</h2><p class="enc-born-p">Born in Corte, in what is now <a href="/countries/france">France</a>. Died in <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/florence">Florence</a>, in what is now <a href="/countries/italy">Italy</a>. <span class="enc-born-src">Places as Wikidata records them.</span></p><figure class="st-locator enc-loc"><a href="/atlas?at=42.31,9.15&amp;year=1817" aria-label="Corte on the atlas"><svg class="map map-globe map-loc" viewBox="0 0 10000 10000" role="img" aria-label="Globe: the birthplace of Joseph Bonaparte"><image href="/maps/globes/g000n30.svg" x="0" y="0" width="10000" height="10000"/><path class="map-dot-halo" d="M5576 3933h0"/><path class="map-dot-me" d="M5576 3933h0"/></svg></a><figcaption>The dot marks the birthplace. <a href="/atlas?at=42.31,9.15&amp;year=1817">What was here in 1817? &rarr;</a></figcaption></figure></section>
